ce = current_dist + weight\n                \n                if distance < distances.get(neighbor, float('inf')):\n                    distances[neighbor] = distance\n                    previous[neighbor] = current_node\n                    heapq.heappush(pq, (distance, neighbor))\n    \n    # Check if end is reachable\n    if end not in distances:\n        return (None, None)\n    \n    # Reconstruct path\n    path = []\n    current = end\n    while current is not None:\n        path.append(current)\n        current = previous.get(current)\n    \n    path.reverse()\n    \n    return (distances[end], path)\n```\n\nI'll continue tracing the algorithm's logic for handling graph traversal and path reconstruction. The key steps involve using a priority queue to efficiently find the shortest path, tracking distances and previous nodes for backtracking when a destination is found.\n\nThe implementation carefully manages edge cases like unreachable nodes and negative weight detection before running Dijkstra's algorithm.",
